implementationPlan.description:
Final adversarial self-review, once (load adversarial-self-review), scoped to the four-file diff.

Mutation proof in a scratch copy only, never the checkout. Apply each mutation, run the HA rows and CJ13, confirm it is killed, then restore:
(1) classify by code string, skipping lockfileRefusal: CJ13 and HA3 (a)-(c) fail;
(2) record INPUT_INVALID too: HA4 fails;
(3) set exitCode to null: HA1 and HA5 fail;
(4) read error.code instead of the recorded code: HA3 (e) fails;
(5) drop run.refusal: HA1 and HA5 fail;
(6) build a tail from error.message or the lockfile dir: HA1 fails;
(7) add an abort check before returning the refusal: HA2 fails.

Checklist:
- Concurrency: run.refusal is per run, and the WeakMap is keyed by the error object.
- Hostile values: WeakMap.get never throws or calls a Proxy trap.
- No test reaches the network.
- Cross-platform: no path logic is added, and the HA rows are pure JS that also run on Windows.
- Only the four files changed.
- Untouched: local-runner.test.mjs, CERTIFICATION.md, repairPolicy.ts, check-dispatch.mjs, check-evidence.mjs and guest-supervisor.py.
- No forbidden pattern.

If the review changes code, rerun the exact gate.

In complete_task, report:
- the mutation results;
- the code-to-outcome matrix;
- that the landed check-dispatch still records no pre-dispatch stop (runOnce !dispatch), so the daemon sees this result only after a future row records it.

Post a task comment @governor-693b7073 for CQ-R13: supervisorSha256 and checkEnvironment are unchanged, and runCheckJob's refusal contract changed. The wrapper lands the work; QA reviews.
